Hello everyone,

I wanted to share a quick note on our experience using Quarkus as the =
framework for our AWS Lambda functions, with SnapStart enabled, =
leveraging the CRaC API. We've seen significant improvements in cold =
startup times, which has been great for our use case.

However, we've encountered an issue during cold starts where we =
consistently see a "connection to node xx terminated" exception related =
to our use of SmallRye Reactive Messaging with the Apache Kafka =
connector. Although the Kafka client automatically reconnects and =
retries, and we haven't lost any messages, this exception can be =
misleading and cause unnecessary concern. Initially, I suspected a =
misconfiguration and spent time investigating potential network issues =
(firewalls, subnets, etc.).

I attempted to close or flush Kafka client connections in the =
beforeCheckpoint method to avoid old connections being reused when the =
snapshot is reloaded, but unfortunately, this didn't resolve the issue.

My key takeaway is that while CRaC support might not be widespread =
across all JDK distributions yet, there are still many systems that can =
benefit significantly from it.

Best regards,
Michael

=20

On 2023/04/20 12:22:13 Radim Vansa wrote:=20
> Hello,=20
>=20
> upon filing a PR [1] with some initial support for OpenJDK CRaC [2][3] =
I=20
> was directed here to raise a KIP (I don't have the permissions in=20
> wiki/JIRA to create the KIP page yet, though).=20
>=20
> In a nutshell, CRaC intends to provide a way to checkpoint (snapshot)=20
> and persist a running Java application and later restore it, possibly =
on=20
> a different computer. This can be used to significantly speed up the=20
> boot process (from seconds or minutes to tens of milliseconds), live=20
> replication or migration of the heated up application. This is not=20
> entirely transparent to the application; the application can register=20
> for notification when this is happening, and sometime has to assist =
with=20
> that to prevent unexpected state after restore - e.g. close network=20
> connections and files.=20
>=20
> CRaC is not integrated yet into the mainline JDK; JEP is being =
prepared,=20
> and users are welcome to try out our builds. However even when this =
gets=20
> into JDK we can't expect users jump onto the latest release =
immediately;=20
> therefore we provide a facade package org.crac [4] that delegates to =
the=20
> implementation, if it is present in the running JDK, or provides a =
no-op=20
> implementation.=20
>=20
> With or without the implementation, the support for CRaC in the=20
> application should be designed to have a minimal impact on performance =

> (few extra objects, some volatile reads...). On the other hand the=20
> checkpoint operation itself can be non-trivial in this matter. =
Therefore=20
> the main consideration should be about the maintenance costs - keeping =
a=20
> small JAR in dependencies and some extra code in networking and =
persistence.=20
>=20
> The support for CRaC does not have to be all-in for all components -=20
> maybe it does not make sense to snapshot a Broker. My PR was for Kafka =

> Clients because the open network connections need to be handled in a =
web=20
> application (in my case I am enabling CRaC in Quarkus Superheros [5]=20
> demo). The PR does not handle all possible client-side uses; as I am =
not=20
> familiar with Kafka I follow the whack-a-mole strategy.=20
>=20
> It is possible that the C/R could be handled in a different layer, =
e.g.=20
> in Quarkus integration code. However our intent is to push the changes =

> as low in the technology stack as possible, to provide the best fanout =

> to users without duplicating maintenance efforts. Also having the=20
> support higher up can be fragile and break encapsulation.=20
>=20
> Thank you for your consideration, I hope that you'll appreciate our=20
> attempt to innovate the Java ecosystem.=20
>=20
> Radim Vansa=20
>=20
> PS: I'd appreciate if someone could give me the permissions on wiki to =

> create a proper KIP! Username: rvansa (both Confluence and JIRA).=20
