>> Thanks for the KIP!
>>=20
>> This is not my area, but I have some questions/comments:
>>=20
>> 1. CMIIW, but I believe that specifying this custom parser class shou=
ld be done via configuration, not system properties or environment varia=
bles, right?
> There are several considerations that were involved here.
> 1. Currently ProduceRequest.parse() is a static method, and as such ha=
s=20
> access only to static members of the class.
> 2. I found no precedent in Kafka for using configs in static=20
> initializers. I did find precedents for props and envs.
> 3. Static members are initialized during the class load time and do no=
t=20
> yet have access to instances of the configuration class.
> 4. Kafka can be run in multiple ways and in multiple environments that=20
> pass configuration differently from just files. Docker and K8s envs=20
> pass configs with env vars.
>
> Ultimately, above was my reasoning for doing it via props and envs. If=20
> there is a better suggestion in this scenario, I'll adopt the code. I=20
> just do not currently see how this is possible.
>
>> 2. What happens if the custom parser can=E2=80=99t be loaded? Say the=
 class isn=E2=80=99t found on the classpath, etc.?
> Custom parsers are rather advanced and require quite a bit of Kafka=20
> internals knowledge. As such, only advanced users would be using this=20
> feature.=20
> If such an advanced user is specifying a custom parser class that is=20
> either unavailable or has problems Kafka will fail to process requests=20
> and report the errors in the log file.=20
> The user will then be able to troubleshoot and adjust their=20
> configuration as appropriate.
>
>> 3. What is the behavior around exception handling? Right now the pars=
er handles malformed data, but what if the custom parser wants to enforc=
e a policy requirement?
> The whole purpose of allowing the custom parser is to allow users to=20
> build one for their own use=E2=80=93cases. Each use-case is unique and=
 we can't=20
> enforce it on a broker level. If the custom parser will not handle, bu=
t=20
> throw an exception, the exception will be handled the way it is handle=
d=20
> currently. The error will be reported and the request will be dropped.
>
>> 4. Given the above, I do believe there are some additional cases that=
 deserve mentioning in the test plan section.
> I presume you mean to test for a custom class specified via props, via=20
> envs, being absent, throwing exceptions at init, throwing exceptions a=
t=20
> parse time?
> I'll look into creating these.=20
> Thanks,
> Max
>
>>=20
>> Thanks,
>> Kirk
