I totally understand the burden of maintaining features going forward. =
The backward compatibility breaking behavior you are describing is a =
normal part of software evolution, just like deprecation. The potential =
for having to break backward compatibility sometime in the future is =
hardly a reason not to add features today. With this reasoning we'd =
never add any features :) Any of them may change in the future :)



> On Aug 29, 2024, at 2:36 PM, Colin McCabe <cmccabe@apache.org> wrote:
>=20
> Hi Maxim,
>=20
> The issue is not the number of lines of code, but the need to support =
this indefinitely in the future. For example, if we want to move to a =
new request type that is different than ProduceRequest, or create a new =
version of ProduceRequest, we wouldn't be able to do that in the future =
while maintaining the compatibility promised in this KIP. So this would =
be a very significant constraint on development, which we probably =
wouldn't adopt unless there was no other way forward.
>=20
> best,
> Colin
>=20
> On Thu, Aug 29, 2024, at 11:27, Maxim Fortun wrote:
>> Hi Colin,
>> Thank you for the background. As a new user of Kafka I appreciate =
being=20
>> caught up. I tried looking for this in PRs and KIPs but did not find=20=

>> any references.
>> Since my change is tiny, doesn't really change the way the broker=20
>> works, and only adds an ability to customize the broker without=20
>> actually changing how the broker does things - I am hoping I'll be =
able=20
>> to persuade the community to adopt it.
>> This feature is actually critical for a rather large-scale=20
>> high-throughput high-volume project that the company I work for is=20
>> currently POCing. We are maintaining a fork with this feature, but =
I'd=20
>> prefer not to maintain a fork, and also hoping the community may=20
>> benefit from this.
>> Thanks,
>> Max
>>=20
>>> On Aug 29, 2024, at 2:02 PM, Colin McCabe <cmccabe@apache.org> =
wrote:
